The simplest mental model is:
    SharePoint List ≈ Dataverse Table

In list we can again create 5 different things
1)List
2)Forms
3)Gallery whihc is used to store images
4)Calender which is used to store any events
5)Board like a jira board.

In SharePoint, the main object is simply a List. You can create it in different ways:
        Blank list → build your own columns, similar to creating a Dataverse custom table.
        From a template → for scenarios like issue tracking, work progress, etc.
        From an existing list → copy the structure.
        From Excel/CSV → create a list based on existing tabular data.
But these don't become fundamentally different table types like Activity Table vs Standard Table in Dataverse.

Column type                                Example
Single line of text                       Request Name
Multiple lines of text                Description / Comments
Number                                    Quantity = 10
Currency                                Budget = $25,000
Yes/No                                    Approved = Yes
Choice                            Pending / Approved / Rejected
Date and Time                                Request Date
Person or Group                                Approver
Lookup                                Department from another List
Calculated                            Total = Quantity × Price
Hyperlink                                Project URL    
Managed Metadata                Organization-controlled terms/categories


List Permissions:

A SharePoint List can have its own permissions, just like a Document Library.
Default behavior
Normally:
    Site Permissions
     ↓ inherited
     List Permissions
     ↓ inherited
     List Item Permissions

So if Budget Requests inherits from the site, the same Owners/Members/Visitors access applies.
If you need different security
You can break permission inheritance on the List and assign different users/groups.

Example:
    Budget Approval Site → 50 users have access
    
    But:
        Salary List
         → Break inheritance
         → HR Group → Edit
         → Managers → Read
         → Everyone else → No access
And you can go even deeper:
Site → List → Individual List Item
An individual item can also have unique permissions.

So remember:
Site, List, Document Library, Folder, File, and List Item can all participate in SharePoint's permission model.
The lower levels normally inherit permissions until inheritance is broken.
